**Ruling: outside the shared claim. 5.2 is not reopened, the derived sequence stands, and H0 resumes.** Hydration is a HEAD write that commits no transaction, so the engineer was right to stop on the literal wording. But the stop rule guards one property: the HEAD writes of one directory carry strictly increasing sequences. Hydration cannot break it, because it is only ever the first HEAD write of a directory, and it installs an index at that index's own greatest `t`, exactly as restart recovery does. The design's wording is what is defective. No file was edited and nothing was run; this is from reading the design and the code.

